# Environment Variables Guide

## Optional Environment Variables

Create a `.env.local` file in the root directory with the following variables:

### Social & Contact Links
```
NEXT_PUBLIC_GITHUB_URL=https://github.com/your-username
NEXT_PUBLIC_LINKEDIN_URL=https://linkedin.com/in/your-profile
NEXT_PUBLIC_TWITTER_URL=https://twitter.com/your-username
NEXT_PUBLIC_EMAIL=your.email@example.com
```

### Contact form (Web3Forms)
The contact form has two paths and picks one automatically:

- **Key set** — the form POSTs to Web3Forms and the message lands in your inbox.
- **Key not set** — the form opens the visitor's mail client with the message
  pre-filled instead. It never claims a message was sent when it wasn't.

```
# Free access key from https://web3forms.com (paste the key, no quotes)
NEXT_PUBLIC_WEB3FORMS_KEY=xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx
```

Same mechanism as the SD Services project, so one Web3Forms account covers both.

### Analytics (Optional)
```
# Google Analytics
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX

# Plausible Analytics
NEXT_PUBLIC_PLAUSIBLE_DOMAIN=your-domain.com
```

## Setup Instructions

1. Copy this file content or create `.env.local`
2. Fill in your actual values
3. The project will work without these variables (defaults will be used)
4. Never commit `.env.local` to version control

## Notes

- Variables starting with `NEXT_PUBLIC_` are exposed to the browser
- Private variables are only available on the server side
- The `.env.local` file is in `.gitignore` for security
- Changes to `.env.local` require restarting the dev server

## Example `.env.local`

```
NEXT_PUBLIC_WEB3FORMS_KEY=xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx
```
